Q: How do I use HTML / CSS to have items span a grid if there are fewer items in the row than there are columns? A: <p>The main problem is that <code>display: grid</code> is not the right display mode for what you want to achieve. By design, grids always have equally spaced and equally aligned cells (within their respective rows or columns). Your mockup breaks that rule.</p>
<p><em>Side note: Cells in a grid can span multiple rows or columns, but still have to align with the next column or row regardless.</em></p>
<p>What you want is a different display mode: <code>display: flex</code>. Here is what this could look like, building from your example:</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;style&gt;
    .grid-title {
        border: 1px solid black;
        background-color: var(--navbarbg);
        text-align: center;
    }

    .grid-body {
        display: flex;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
        text-align: center;
    }

    .grid-item {
        min-width: 33%;
        flex: 1;
        border: 1px solid black;
    }

&lt;/style&gt;

&lt;div class=&quot;grid-title&quot;&gt;&lt;h3&gt;Botanicals&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div class=&quot;grid-body&quot;&gt;

    &lt;div class=&quot;grid-item&quot;&gt;
        &lt;img src=&quot;https://placehold.co/400x400&quot; alt=&quot;LEGO Cherry Blossoms Set&quot; style=&quot;width: 10vw&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;
        &lt;a href=&quot;https://rebrickable.com/sets/40725-1/cherry-blossoms/#parts&quot;&gt;Cherry Blossoms&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;div class=&quot;grid-item&quot;&gt;
        &lt;img src=&quot;https://placehold.co/400x400&quot; alt=&quot;LEGO Happy Plants Set&quot; style=&quot;width: 10vw&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;
        &lt;a href=&quot;https://rebrickable.com/sets/10349-1/happy-plants/#parts&quot;&gt;Happy Plants&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;div class=&quot;grid-item&quot;&gt;
        &lt;img src=&quot;https://placehold.co/400x400&quot; alt=&quot;LEGO Lucky Bamboo Set&quot; style=&quot;width: 10vw&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;
        &lt;a href=&quot;https://rebrickable.com/sets/10344-1/lucky-bamboo/#parts&quot;&gt;Lucky Bamboo&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;div class=&quot;grid-item&quot;&gt;
        &lt;img src=&quot;https://placehold.co/400x400&quot; alt=&quot;LEGO Mini Orchid Set&quot; style=&quot;width: 10vw&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;
        &lt;a href=&quot;https://rebrickable.com/sets/10343-1/mini-orchid/#parts&quot;&gt;Mini Orchid&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;div class=&quot;grid-item&quot;&gt;
        &lt;img src=&quot;https://placehold.co/400x400&quot; style=&quot;width: 10vw&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;
        &lt;a href=&quot;https://rebrickable.com/sets/40524-1/sunflowers/#parts&quot;&gt;Sunflowers&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Here are the important bits to change:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>grid-body</code>
<ul>
<li><code>display: flex</code> instead of <code>display: grid</code>. This changes the layout mode from a grid to a flexbox.</li>
<li><code>flex-wrap: wrap</code> Usually, all elements in <code>grid-body</code> would be placed in a single row side by side. This property tells <code>grid-body</code> to just start a new row if the first row does not have enough space for all elements.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><code>grid-item</code>
<ul>
<li><code>min-width: 33%</code> This ensures the elements will <em>at least</em> use a third of <code>grid-body</code>'s width. This means that the fourth element in this container will not have enough space in the first row and will wrap around.</li>
<li><code>flex: 1</code> This will tell <code>grid-body</code> to stretch out this element's width if there is more space available in the current row.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<p>These properties together will create the layout described in your mockup. Essentially, the layouting goes like this:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>grid-body</code> will add the first three elements and give each of them a third of it's width</li>
<li>The fourth and fifth element need to go in a new row because we have run out of space in the first row and <code>flex-wrap</code> is set to <code>wrap</code>. Each element in this row will get a third of the width again.</li>
<li>Because <code>flex: 1</code> is set, the elements will be stretched out if there is still space to fill up. In the first row, there is no room left, so this will make no difference, but in the second row, the elements will stretch out to fill the row.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Other stuff</h2>
<p>Here are some things which have nothing to do with your original question but are still relevant:</p>
<ul>
<li>The <code>id</code> attribute on elements are designed to be able to identify a single element on your page. Each value should only be used a single time. Your solution does work because the browser tries to accommodate for any mistakes, but you should be using <code>class</code> instead. In css, the selector will be <code>.grid-body</code> instead of <code>#grid-body</code>. (<code>#</code> indicates that the following will be an ID, <code>.</code> indicates that the following will be a class).</li>
<li><code>grid-title</code>'s <code>display: grid</code> and <code>grid-template-columns</code> did not do anything, so I removed them.</li>
<li><code>grid-items</code>'s <code>justify-content</code> did not do anything, so I removed it as well.</li>
</ul>

Q: How to avoid page breaks right after or in the middle of section titles in a EPUB book created with Bookdown? A: <p>Thanks for this RE. Moving to an answer because that's easier to share code. First, you have to use the <code>stylesheet</code> argument in <code>bookdown::epub_book</code> + the <code>!important</code> notation to enforce your styles. But even then, some ebook readers like calibre still overwrite your styles. The only thing that works for Calibre is to use a pandoc header-id or class CSS-selector as shown <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/63075155/28479453">here</a>:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>#header h1, 
#header h2, 
#header h3, 
#header h4{ 
  page-break-after: avoid !important;  
  page-break-inside: avoid !important; 
  break-after: avoid-page !important;
  widows: 3 !important; 
  orphans: 3 !important;
}', &quot;style.css&quot;)

</code></pre>
<pre><code># Title Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. {#header}
...

## Subtitle Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. 
...

\newpage
</code></pre>
<p>and in the HTML, this will wrap your entire #Title-DOM in a section with the id = header</p>
<pre><code>&lt;section id=&quot;header&quot; class=&quot;level1&quot; data-number=&quot;1&quot;&gt;
 &lt;h1 data-number=&quot;1&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;header-section-number&quot;&gt;1&lt;/span&gt; Title Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here.&lt;/h1&gt;...
  &lt;section id=&quot;subtitle-lots-of-text-here.-lots-of-text-here.-lots-of-text-here.-lots-of-text-here.-lots-of-text-here.-lots-of-text-here.-lots-of-text-here.-lots-of-text-here.-lots-of-text-here.-lots-of-text-here.&quot; class=&quot;level2&quot; data-number=&quot;1.1&quot;&gt;
   &lt;h2 data-number=&quot;1.1&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;header-section-number&quot;&gt;1.1&lt;/span&gt; Subtitle Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here. Lots of text here.&lt;/h2&gt;...
   &lt;!--chapter:end:000_Book_body.Rmd--&gt;
  &lt;/section&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>We can use this, to apply CSS to only child-objects of #header. And <em>only then</em> Calibre respects our styles.</p>
<h2>Reprex</h2>
<p><strong>renderbook.R</strong></p>
<pre><code>writeLines(text = '
bookdown::pdf_book:
  css: style.css
  config:
    toc:
      before: |
        &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;./&quot;&gt;Book title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
      after: |
        &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/rstudio/bookdown&quot; target=&quot;blank&quot;&gt;Impaginato con bookdown&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;

bookdown::epub_book:
  css: style.css
  toc_depth: 2', '_output.yml')

writeLines(text = '
p.caption {
  color: #777;
  margin-top: 10px;
}
p code {
  white-space: inherit;
}
pre {
  word-break: normal;
  word-wrap: normal;
}
pre code {
  white-space: inherit;
}

.caption { text-align: left; }

/* Apply styles to children of object with id = header */
#header h1, 
#header h2, 
#header h3, 
#header h4 { 
  page-break-after: avoid !important;  
  page-break-inside: avoid !important; 
  break-after: avoid-page !important;
  widows: 3 !important; 
  orphans: 3 !important;
}', &quot;style.css&quot;)

writeLines(
  text = paste(
'---
title: My title
subtitle: My subtitle
author: &quot;Me&quot;
toc-title: Indice
toc_depth: 2
toc: false
output: pdf_book
---
\newpage
\thispagestyle{empty}
This page intentionally blank
\newpage
\thispagestyle{empty}
This page intentionally blank
\newpage
\tableofcontents
\newpage'),
  &quot;index.Rmd&quot;
)

lots &lt;- \(n) paste(rep(&quot;Lots of text here.&quot;, n),collapse = &quot; &quot;)

sections &lt;- c(
  paste(&quot;# Title&quot;, lots(10), &quot;{#header}\n\n&quot;, lots(13), &quot;\n\n&quot;, lots(1), &quot;\n\n&quot;, lots(12), &quot;\n\n&quot;, lots(1), &quot;\n\n&quot;, lots(60)),
  paste(&quot;## Subtitle&quot;, lots(10),  &quot;\n\n&quot;, lots(1), &quot;\n\n&quot;, lots(12), &quot;\n\n&quot;, lots(61), &quot;\n\n&quot;, lots(12)),
  &quot;\\newpage&quot;
)

writeLines(paste(sections, collapse = &quot;\n\n&quot;), &quot;000_Book_body.Rmd&quot;)

# Build EPUB:

bookdown::render_book(
  input = &quot;index.Rmd&quot;,
  bookdown::epub_book(
    fig_width = 5,
    fig_height = 4,
    dev = &quot;png&quot;,
    fig_caption = TRUE,
    number_sections = TRUE,
    toc = FALSE,
    toc_depth = 2,
    stylesheet = &quot;style.css&quot;,
    metadata = NULL,
    chapter_level = 1,
    md_extensions = NULL,
    pandoc_args = NULL,
    template = &quot;default&quot;
  )
)
</code></pre>
<p><strong>File Structure <code>printtree::print_rtree(max_dept = 1)</code></strong></p>
<pre><code>main/
|-- _output.yml
|-- 000_Book_body.Rmd
|-- index.Rmd
|-- renderbook.R
`-- style.css 
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h2>Tests</h2>
<p><a href="https://epub-reader.online/" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>https://epub-reader.online/</code></a>
<a href="https://i.sstatic.net/jd0VMgFd.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/jd0VMgFd.png" alt="epubreader online" /></a></p>
<hr />
<p><a href="https://calibre-ebook.com/download" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Calibre</code></a>, you can see that headers do not get broken across pages anymore!</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/oD91kFA4.gif" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/oD91kFA4.gif" alt="Calibre-Test" /></a></p>
